        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill for two people?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most couples, the Horizon 7.4 AT: a 22 by 60-inch deck, a 350 lb limit, a 3.5 CHP continuous motor, 12 mph and 15% incline, for about $1,500 on Amazon on 24 November 2026. The Horizon T202 is the value pick at about $800 with a 60-inch deck and a 325 lb limit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What weight capacity does a treadmill for two people need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Take the heavier user’s weight and add about 50 lb. The two weights are not added together, because only one person uses it at a time. A 250 lb partner, for example, points to a limit of 300 lb or more; for a heavy runner, leave more margin.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which treadmills have user profiles?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Fewer than you might expect. Of the six here, only the Schwinn 810 listing names console user profiles (two). NordicTrack shares through an iFIT household membership and BowFlex through JRNY accounts. Many couples simply track their own sessions in a phone or watch app.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a 55-inch treadmill long enough for two people?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For two walkers of average height, usually yes. If either of you is over about 5 ft 8 in. and jogs or runs, choose a 60-inch deck, because stride lengthens with height and speed. A 55-inch deck is the compromise that keeps the Schwinn 810 under $600.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does sharing a treadmill wear it out faster?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, in proportion to the hours. Two people at 30 minutes a day each give a treadmill about 365 hours a year, so belt lubrication and tension checks come round twice as often. Choose a continuous motor rating and a long motor warranty for a shared machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can two people use a treadmill at the same time?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Home treadmills are built and rated for one user at a time, and the weight limit is for a single person. Two people on the belt together is a fall risk and can overload the motor and deck. Take turns, and agree a schedule if you both train at the same time of day.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

